# HomeAgent hub Buildroot config — Milk-V Duo S / SG2000, ARM A53 boot, eMMC.
# Committed SSOT: bsp/build.sh copies this over buildroot/configs/<board>_defconfig
# before building. Diff vs the stock SDK config is exactly the three blocks below.
#
# 1) TOOLCHAIN — the reason this file exists.
#    Stock is Linaro GCC 7.3.1 / glibc 2.25 / kernel headers 4.10, from 2018. Buildroot's
#    nodejs package requires BR2_TOOLCHAIN_GCC_AT_LEAST_10 (Node 22 BUILDING.md: "Linux |
#    GCC >= 10.1"), so BR2_PACKAGE_NODEJS cannot even be selected against it, and official
#    Node arm64 binaries need glibc >= 2.28 so a prebuilt drop-in is out too. Note the
#    vendor shipped the riscv lanes a 2022 GCC 10.2 and left arm on 2018 — this is an arm
#    lane accident, not a Node problem.
#    Bootlin's prebuilt aarch64 glibc toolchain (GCC 13, headers 4.19, auto-downloaded by
#    Buildroot) clears the gate with room for Node 24 later (GCC >= 12.2). It is a
#    defconfig swap: no toolchain fork, no source patch.
#    WHY GCC 13, and why that is not "less safe" than 12:
#      BR2_TOOLCHAIN_GCC_AT_LEAST_* are FLOORS on a cascading ladder, not feature flags --
#      toolchain/Config.in has _13 select _12 select _11 select _10. Selecting 13 therefore
#      sets 12 as well, and every `depends on BR2_TOOLCHAIN_GCC_AT_LEAST_12` in the tree is
#      satisfied. Grepping the tree finds many references to _12 and none to _13 simply
#      because some arch/package demands *at least* 12 (riscv ISA, one ARM CPU) while
#      nothing yet demands 13+. Fewer hits means more headroom, not less support.
#      Confirmed in the generated .config: _10 _11 _12 _13 are all =y, GCC_AT_LEAST="13".
#      There is also no GCC 12 option for aarch64 to fall back to. The whole menu is:
#        Bootlin glibc stable        GCC 13, headers 4.19  <- chosen
#        Bootlin glibc bleeding-edge GCC 14, headers 5.15  <- rejected, see below
#        Arm AArch64 14.2.rel1       GCC 14, headers 4.20  <- fine, switch here if 14 is ever needed
#      Going below 13 would mean building our own toolchain, which is a new maintenance
#      burden rather than a defconfig swap.
#    WHY NOT the bleeding-edge (GCC 14) Bootlin toolchain: it declares kernel headers 5.15
#      and this board runs a 5.10 kernel. Userspace built against newer headers can reference
#      syscalls/ioctls/structs the running kernel does not have. Buildroot does NOT enforce
#      this -- check_kernel_headers_version only verifies the toolchain is self-consistent --
#      so it would fail at runtime, not at build time. Arm's 14.2 (headers 4.20) is the safe
#      way to GCC 14 if we ever want it.
#    GOTCHA: BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_BOOTLIN_AARCH64_GLIBC_STABLE is nested under the parent
#    BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_BOOTLIN. Setting only the child is silently ignored and Buildroot
#    falls back to the aarch64 default (BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_ARM_AARCH64, GCC 14) — which
#    also builds, so nothing fails and the config just quietly means something else. Both
#    lines are required. Verify after any edit:
#      make -C buildroot O=<out> milkv-duos-glibc-arm64-emmc_defconfig
#      grep -E '^BR2_TOOLCHAIN_GCC_AT_LEAST=|BOOTLIN_AARCH64_GLIBC_STABLE=' <out>/.config
#    Kernel/u-boot are unaffected — envsetup_milkv.sh sets CROSS_COMPILE_64 from
#    host-tools independently of Buildroot's target toolchain.
#
# 2) ROOTFS OVERLAY — adds our overlays on top of the SDK's board overlay, so things like
#    the wlan0 autostart script are baked in instead of hand-applied after every flash.
#    /bsp is where bsp/build.sh mounts this repo's bsp/ inside the container.
#    The list is split by profile, not by topic:
#      overlay/common  files BOTH profiles ship (stable MAC, serial by-id, wpa_supplicant)
#      overlay/z2m     the hub application half (Z2M init + seed config, mosquitto)
#    This file IS the `full` profile and carries both. The `minimal` profile drops the
#    packages AND this second overlay together — an init script for a binary that is not
#    in the image is a boot-time error, not a harmless leftover. See PROFILES below.
#
# PROFILES — one board, two package sets (HOMEAGENT_BSP_PROFILE, bsp/build.sh):
#    full     (default)  this file as-is: Node 22 + Zigbee2MQTT + mosquitto. The hub image.
#    minimal             this file + profiles/<board>_minimal.fragment appended, which
#                        turns Node/ICU/Z2M/mosquitto off. Kconfig takes the LAST value
#                        for a symbol, so the fragment overrides rather than conflicts.
#                        Use it to prove the BSP surface (hostapd, stable MAC, serial
#                        by-id) without paying V8 — measured 1h16m of a 1h29m laptop
#                        build. It is NOT a hub image and must not be flashed as one.
#
# 3) PACKAGES — Node.js 22.22.0 (Buildroot's pinned version; satisfies Zigbee2MQTT
#    2.10.1's "node": "^20.15.0 || ^22.2.0 || ^24") plus system ICU so Intl works.
#    BR2_PACKAGE_NODEJS_NPM stays off, which makes Buildroot pass --without-npm.
#    Corepack is still installed on arm: our patch 0002 scopes --without-corepack to the
#    riscv branch. Harmless without npm; worth widening when patch 0002 is next touched.

BR2_aarch64=y
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL=y
